## Incident 3 (P1): literal-phrase grep fails across PDF line breaks

**What happened.** `muck grep "Libyan Stock Market"` returned only directory-listing hits,
missing the key resolution — because the source PDF renders it as `"Libyan \r\nStock Market"`.
Meanwhile `muck verify` happily verified a quote spanning that same break, because
`cite.py:82` normalizes `\s+` → single space. The two tools disagree about whitespace, and
the strict one is the discovery tool — the worst place for strictness.

**Suggested fix.** In `SqliteStore.grep`, translate literal space runs in the pattern to
`\s+` before matching (both in the `regexp()` UDF filter and the per-row `re.search`), or add
a `--phrase` flag that does so. This makes grep's matching semantics consistent with verify's
quote normalization. PDF-extracted text guarantees mid-phrase `\r\n`; every phrase search
against a parsed-PDF corpus currently has silent false negatives.

**Acceptance test.** `muck grep "Libyan Stock Market"` on this corpus must return the
`Admin_Report_for_2017` chunk (`f2035af77c6.0@1528-3479`).

